WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.195
Welcome to Module 5, the last
module of the Azure Security

2
00:00:04.195 --> 00:00:08.163
Engineering learning path:
policy, drift and evidence.

3
00:00:08.163 --> 00:00:11.737
Everything you engineered in
Modules 1 to 4 can drift.

4
00:00:11.737 --> 00:00:14.876
Policy holds it in place
and reports what drifted;

5
00:00:14.876 --> 00:00:19.162
the operating model gives each control
an owner, a cadence and a test;

6
00:00:19.162 --> 00:00:23.601
and the evidence catalog turns both
into what an assessor can inspect.

7
00:00:23.601 --> 00:00:28.850
This module covers Steps 9 and 10 of the
Azure Security Engineering implementation

8
00:00:28.850 --> 00:00:34.160
guide, and takes about fifty minutes, plus
a lab in your own test management group.

9
00:00:37.910 --> 00:00:39.060
Why does this matter?

10
00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:43.561
Governance, Drift and Evidence makes the
case that drift is the primary threat,

11
00:00:43.561 --> 00:00:46.885
and the guide's dangers table
shows what it looks like here.

12
00:00:46.885 --> 00:00:50.156
A new public I P appears in
a workload subscription,

13
00:00:50.156 --> 00:00:53.973
outside DDoS protection and
the web application firewall.

14
00:00:53.973 --> 00:00:57.035
A firewall policy gets
switched back to detection.

15
00:00:57.035 --> 00:00:59.685
A vault is created with public access on.

16
00:00:59.685 --> 00:01:03.760
Nothing you engineered in Modules
1 to 4 stays put on its own.

17
00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:04.948
Three things hold it.

18
00:01:04.948 --> 00:01:10.022
Policy: one security initiative,
enforced, with exemptions that expire.

19
00:01:10.022 --> 00:01:14.748
Operations: an owner, a cadence
and a test for every control.

20
00:01:14.748 --> 00:01:17.073
And evidence an assessor can inspect.

21
00:01:17.073 --> 00:01:21.695
This module turns Steps 3 to 8 into
policy, runs them, measures coverage

22
00:01:21.695 --> 00:01:27.170
rather than settings, and gets each
finding ready for the assessor's decision.

23
00:01:30.920 --> 00:01:33.833
Here's what you'll be able to
do by the end of this module.

24
00:01:33.833 --> 00:01:37.287
You'll gather the security
definitions into one initiative,

25
00:01:37.287 --> 00:01:41.157
and move it from do-not-enforce to
default after a compliance review.

26
00:01:41.157 --> 00:01:45.845
You'll explain what a remediation task
does, and what stays after a rollback.

27
00:01:45.845 --> 00:01:50.820
You'll create an exemption with a category
and an expiry, and find the ones past it.

28
00:01:50.820 --> 00:01:55.807
You'll run the DDoS incident runbook and
the recurring reviews, and read the RACI.

29
00:01:55.807 --> 00:01:59.274
And you'll compute the guide's
metrics as protected coverage,

30
00:01:59.274 --> 00:02:02.670
not settings, and say what makes
a finding ready for closure.

31
00:02:02.670 --> 00:02:06.040
You need Modules 1 to 4,
and a skim of two chapters:

32
00:02:06.040 --> 00:02:12.089
Chapter 05 of Cloud Boundary and Landing
Zone, on enforcing without breaking things,

33
00:02:12.089 --> 00:02:15.910
and Chapter 02 of Governance,
Drift and Evidence.

34
00:02:19.660 --> 00:02:21.872
Unit 1 is the guide's Step 9.

35
00:02:21.872 --> 00:02:26.400
Gather the security definitions from
Steps 3 to 8 into one initiative,

36
00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:31.863
assigned at the intermediate root: DDoS
protection on virtual networks and its logs,

37
00:02:31.863 --> 00:02:36.156
the firewall-enabled and firewall-mode
policies that Learn's page,

38
00:02:36.156 --> 00:02:40.058
Azure Web Application Firewall
and Azure Policy, describes,

39
00:02:40.058 --> 00:02:44.585
the private D N S and public-access
policies, and the Key Vault policies.

40
00:02:44.585 --> 00:02:46.930
Built-in definitions differ by cloud,

41
00:02:46.930 --> 00:02:50.335
so look each one up by display
name in the cloud you're in.

42
00:02:50.335 --> 00:02:53.572
Assign the initiative in
do-not-enforce mode first.

43
00:02:53.572 --> 00:02:58.761
Policies with deploy-if-not-exists or modify
effects need a managed identity and a

44
00:02:58.761 --> 00:03:02.119
role; grant the narrowest
role each definition names,

45
00:03:02.119 --> 00:03:05.172
not Contributor, where
the initiative allows it.

46
00:03:05.172 --> 00:03:08.432
After at least one evaluation
cycle and a review of

47
00:03:08.432 --> 00:03:11.847
the non-compliant list with
owners, switch to Default.

48
00:03:11.847 --> 00:03:15.846
Then run a remediation task
for each deploy-if-not-exists

49
00:03:15.846 --> 00:03:19.260
or modify policy, to
fix what already exists.

50
00:03:23.010 --> 00:03:24.472
Exemptions expire.

51
00:03:24.472 --> 00:03:29.122
Learn's page on the Azure Policy
exemption structure gives two categories:

52
00:03:29.122 --> 00:03:32.422
Mitigated, when the policy's
intent is met another way,

53
00:03:32.422 --> 00:03:35.722
and Waiver, when non-compliance
is accepted for a time.

54
00:03:35.722 --> 00:03:40.084
Every exemption gets an expiry and
a reference to its decision record.

55
00:03:40.084 --> 00:03:44.805
And once it expires, Learn says it's kept
for the record but no longer honored,

56
00:03:44.805 --> 00:03:47.622
so the resource shows
as non-compliant again.

57
00:03:47.622 --> 00:03:52.735
The guide adds a rule: exemptions
only, never not-scopes for convenience.

58
00:03:52.735 --> 00:03:55.755
Each month, export
compliance: the summary,

59
00:03:55.755 --> 00:03:59.897
and the list of non-compliant
resources, filed as evidence.

60
00:03:59.897 --> 00:04:02.696
Rollback, if enforcement breaks something,

61
00:04:02.696 --> 00:04:05.960
is the assignment back to
do-not-enforce, in minutes.

62
00:04:05.960 --> 00:04:11.720
One caution: resources a remediation task
created stay until someone removes them.

63
00:04:15.470 --> 00:04:19.082
Unit 2 is the guide's Step
10, the operating model.

64
00:04:19.082 --> 00:04:23.745
Its DDoS runbook is design guidance,
built on Learn's response strategy.

65
00:04:23.745 --> 00:04:26.809
The under-attack alert
pages the security monitor,

66
00:04:26.809 --> 00:04:29.945
and Defender for Cloud
raises the mitigation alert.

67
00:04:29.945 --> 00:04:32.173
The monitor checks application health,

68
00:04:32.173 --> 00:04:34.940
and the firewall logs for
a layer seven component,

69
00:04:34.940 --> 00:04:39.598
with the top-talker queries from Learn's
page on application layer DDoS protection;

70
00:04:39.598 --> 00:04:44.120
the platform owner lowers the rate limits
through an emergency change if needed.

71
00:04:44.120 --> 00:04:45.933
If mitigation isn't enough,

72
00:04:45.933 --> 00:04:50.795
open a severity A request for Rapid
Response, under Network Protection.

73
00:04:50.795 --> 00:04:53.911
Afterwards: the mitigation
logs, the timeline,

74
00:04:53.911 --> 00:04:57.520
any cost-protection claim,
and an after-action report.

75
00:04:57.520 --> 00:05:02.526
The review calendar is design guidance
too: the exposure register weekly;

76
00:05:02.526 --> 00:05:07.778
compliance, exemptions and firewall
false positives monthly; coverage, cost,

77
00:05:07.778 --> 00:05:11.552
firewall rules, vault access
and the rule set quarterly;

78
00:05:11.552 --> 00:05:16.558
failover and a vault restore twice a
year; and a DDoS simulation every year.

79
00:05:16.558 --> 00:05:19.300
The guide's RACI names who does each.

80
00:05:23.050 --> 00:05:27.438
Measure protected coverage and
effectiveness, not that a setting exists.

81
00:05:27.438 --> 00:05:29.762
The guide's metrics are design guidance,

82
00:05:29.762 --> 00:05:33.213
and each divides what's protected
and tested by what exists.

83
00:05:33.213 --> 00:05:36.671
Exposure ownership: register
rows with an owner,

84
00:05:36.671 --> 00:05:39.438
over all rows, target a hundred percent.

85
00:05:39.438 --> 00:05:43.492
DDoS coverage: public I Ps with
Network or I P Protection,

86
00:05:43.492 --> 00:05:46.577
over public I Ps,
excluding NAT gateway I Ps.

87
00:05:46.577 --> 00:05:52.747
Web application firewall prevention coverage,
origin lock-down, egress inspection,

88
00:05:52.747 --> 00:05:57.859
private pass, vault posture, policy
compliance, and exemption hygiene,

89
00:05:57.859 --> 00:06:01.825
where the target is zero
exemptions past their expiry.

90
00:06:01.825 --> 00:06:02.912
Then closure.

91
00:06:02.912 --> 00:06:08.219
A finding the guide addresses is ready
for closure when every applicable control

92
00:06:08.219 --> 00:06:12.199
state, Designed, Configured,
Enforced, Evidenced and Operated,

93
00:06:12.199 --> 00:06:16.101
has evidence; the authorizing
official or assessor decides.

94
00:06:16.101 --> 00:06:20.975
Findings from a Zero Trust assessment
enter the closure chain that Zero Trust

95
00:06:20.975 --> 00:06:26.060
and Assessment sets out, weighted by
exposure and blast radius, not pass rate.

96
00:06:29.810 --> 00:06:31.122
Where are agencies?

97
00:06:31.122 --> 00:06:35.695
In G C C, the workbook's baseline, the
subscriptions are commercial Azure,

98
00:06:35.695 --> 00:06:39.260
and the commercial subscriptions
run Step 9 as written.

99
00:06:39.260 --> 00:06:43.788
Azure Policy is available there, with
the fed-ramp initiatives built in,

100
00:06:43.788 --> 00:06:46.398
as Cloud Boundary and Landing Zone notes.

101
00:06:46.398 --> 00:06:50.459
But built-in definitions and the
fed-ramp initiatives change version.

102
00:06:50.459 --> 00:06:53.173
That's why the guide lists
them by display name,

103
00:06:53.173 --> 00:06:56.385
and has you look each one up
where you are, as the lab does.

104
00:06:56.385 --> 00:06:59.716
And a test that can't run because
the cloud doesn't offer the

105
00:06:59.716 --> 00:07:04.040
feature is recorded as an environment
decision, not a failed control.

106
00:07:07.790 --> 00:07:11.490
The lab exports the evidence Step
9 files each month, from a test

107
00:07:11.490 --> 00:07:16.115
management group where someone has
assigned the guide's security initiative.

108
00:07:16.115 --> 00:07:20.350
Every command reads, the only thing
written is a folder on your machine,

109
00:07:20.350 --> 00:07:22.102
and the authors haven't run it.

110
00:07:22.102 --> 00:07:26.013
Step one shows the initiative's
enforcement mode: do-not-enforce

111
00:07:26.013 --> 00:07:30.291
during the audit phase, Default once
the compliance review has passed.

112
00:07:30.291 --> 00:07:34.178
Step two looks up one built-in
definition in the cloud you're in.

113
00:07:34.178 --> 00:07:39.528
Step three exports this month's compliance:
the summary and the non-compliant list.

114
00:07:39.528 --> 00:07:44.103
The guide's Validate step says the
summary shows the initiative's compliance.

115
00:07:44.103 --> 00:07:48.106
Step four lists the exemptions with
their category and expiry, using the

116
00:07:48.106 --> 00:07:52.577
management-group example from Learn's
reference for the exemption list command.

117
00:07:52.577 --> 00:07:56.896
Expect no exemption past its
expiry without a renewal decision,

118
00:07:56.896 --> 00:07:59.040
and none without an expiry.

119
00:08:02.790 --> 00:08:07.128
Step five computes two of the guide's
metrics from the exposure register.

120
00:08:07.128 --> 00:08:11.053
It reruns Module 1's public
I P query, then counts.

121
00:08:11.053 --> 00:08:15.052
Exposure ownership is the rows
with an owner over all rows.

122
00:08:15.052 --> 00:08:18.162
DDoS coverage is the
rows whose DDoS mode is

123
00:08:18.162 --> 00:08:21.877
Enabled or Virtual Network
Inherited, over all rows.

124
00:08:21.877 --> 00:08:26.777
The arithmetic is the path's own; the
definitions are the guide's metrics table.

125
00:08:26.777 --> 00:08:29.832
Exclude public I Ps on
NAT gateways by hand,

126
00:08:29.832 --> 00:08:35.328
as the DDoS coverage metric says, because
DDoS protection doesn't cover them.

127
00:08:35.328 --> 00:08:38.164
The guide's target for
both is a hundred percent.

128
00:08:38.164 --> 00:08:39.703
Step six backs it out.

129
00:08:39.703 --> 00:08:41.865
The lab created nothing in Azure.

130
00:08:41.865 --> 00:08:45.299
If this is a real review, file
the exports with their date,

131
00:08:45.299 --> 00:08:50.152
the cloud and subscription, and the tester,
as the guide's evidence pattern asks.

132
00:08:50.152 --> 00:08:52.530
Otherwise, delete the folder.

133
00:08:56.280 --> 00:09:01.335
The federal requirement, from NIST special
publication eight hundred fifty-three,

134
00:09:01.335 --> 00:09:04.705
revision five, as the guide's
requirements table maps it.

135
00:09:04.705 --> 00:09:10.480
Step 9 supports C M two, baseline
configuration, C M six, configuration settings,

136
00:09:10.480 --> 00:09:13.146
and C A seven, continuous monitoring;

137
00:09:13.146 --> 00:09:18.655
its evidence is the monthly compliance
export and the exemption register.

138
00:09:18.655 --> 00:09:24.686
Step 10 supports I R four, incident
handling, C P four, contingency plan testing,

139
00:09:24.686 --> 00:09:29.775
and A U six, audit record review;
its evidence is the review records,

140
00:09:29.775 --> 00:09:33.167
the exercises, and the
after-action reports.

141
00:09:33.167 --> 00:09:36.822
The guide notes that those controls
ask for incident handling,

142
00:09:36.822 --> 00:09:40.687
contingency plan testing and
continuous monitoring at Moderate,

143
00:09:40.687 --> 00:09:43.006
and that the cadence is the program's own.

144
00:09:43.006 --> 00:09:45.740
Each row lines up with the fed-ramp twenty

145
00:09:45.740 --> 00:09:48.555
X key security indicators the guide names.

146
00:09:48.555 --> 00:09:54.080
The steps support these controls; the
assessor decides whether each is satisfied.

147
00:09:57.830 --> 00:09:59.805
Check yourself with two questions.

148
00:09:59.805 --> 00:10:03.117
First: an exemption's
expiry date has passed.

149
00:10:03.117 --> 00:10:04.130
What happens?

150
00:10:04.130 --> 00:10:08.000
The answer is b: it's kept for
the record but no longer honored,

151
00:10:08.000 --> 00:10:11.555
and the guide reports expired
exemptions every month.

152
00:10:11.555 --> 00:10:15.530
Second: when is a finding this
guide addresses ready for closure?

153
00:10:15.530 --> 00:10:19.905
The answer is b: when every applicable
control state has evidence.

154
00:10:19.905 --> 00:10:23.084
The authorizing official
or assessor decides;

155
00:10:23.084 --> 00:10:25.855
a pass in an assessment
isn't the decision.

156
00:10:25.855 --> 00:10:31.130
The module page has three more questions,
each linked to the unit that answers it.

157
00:10:34.880 --> 00:10:35.705
To sum up.

158
00:10:35.705 --> 00:10:38.539
You checked the security
initiative's enforcement

159
00:10:38.539 --> 00:10:40.780
mode and exported the month's compliance.

160
00:10:40.780 --> 00:10:44.105
You listed the exemptions with
their category and expiry.

161
00:10:44.105 --> 00:10:47.192
You walked the DDoS runbook
and the review calendar.

162
00:10:47.192 --> 00:10:50.239
And you computed two of the
guide's metrics as coverage,

163
00:10:50.239 --> 00:10:54.368
the kind of evidence that gets a finding
ready for the assessor's decision.

164
00:10:54.368 --> 00:10:57.170
The evidence an I S S O
keeps from this module:

165
00:10:57.170 --> 00:11:02.617
the initiative and assignment exports, the
monthly compliance exports, deny events,

166
00:11:02.617 --> 00:11:07.753
the exemption register with its decision
records, the RACI and review calendar,

167
00:11:07.753 --> 00:11:09.621
review and exercise records,

168
00:11:09.621 --> 00:11:13.668
and accepted risks with the
authorizing official's signature.

169
00:11:13.668 --> 00:11:15.167
That completes the path.

170
00:11:15.167 --> 00:11:20.046
The guide's implementation checklist
puts all ten steps on one page, and the

171
00:11:20.046 --> 00:11:25.610
developer workbook, Building Azure Security,
takes every step into labs on fixtures.
